<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Privacy manifest for the Moshpit app. App Store submissions are checked for
  one of these whenever the binary touches a "required reason" API, and the
  declaration has to match what the code actually does.

  Moshpit reaches exactly one such category: UserDefaults. Two reasons apply.
    CA92.1 — UserDefaults.standard, read/written only by the app itself
             (AppSettings, ConnectionStore, ThemeStore, AppThemeStore,
             SSHKeyStore, TerminalShortcut, HostCapabilities).
    1C8F.1 — UserDefaults(suiteName: "group.com.cluas.moshpit"), shared with
             the MoshpitIsland widget extension in the same App Group
             (Island/AgentWidgetState.swift).

  Deliberately absent, because a grep of the sources finds no use of them:
  file timestamps, disk space, system boot time, and active keyboards. Add the
  matching category here if that ever changes — a missing declaration is
  rejected at upload, and a stale over-declaration is its own kind of lie.

  Nothing is collected and nothing is tracked: SSH credentials live in the
  local Keychain, everything else in local UserDefaults, and there is no
  Moshpit server for any of it to be sent to.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>CA92.1</string>
				<string>1C8F.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
